# Target C — repair of the consecutive-tooth ray-order step

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**Scope:** proof-only repair for `target_c_consecutive_tooth_turn_budget_2026-09-12.md`.
**Disposition:** the stated turn inequality survives, but the original ordering sentence and the first hostile audit are insufficient as written.

The original proof says that because the two tooth-apex rays lie in the common interior
wedge at `p_{i+1}`, their order is fixed. That implication is false: two rays can lie
in the same wedge in either order. The missing input is unit separation between the
first tooth apex and the **next loaded hull vertex**.

Keep the notation of the theorem. Direct the preceding hull side
`p_i p_{i+1}` at argument zero. Put

\[
\beta_i=\arccos(b_i/2),
\qquad
\beta_{i+1}=\arccos(b_{i+1}/2).
\]

The first inward tooth ray from `p_{i+1}` has argument

\[
\theta_i=\pi-\beta_i,
\]

while the next loaded hull side has argument `alpha_i` and the next inward tooth ray
has argument

\[
\theta_{i+1}=\alpha_i+\beta_{i+1}.
\]

Inwardness gives

\[
\alpha_i\le\theta_i,\theta_{i+1}\le\pi.
\]

We claim

\[
\boxed{\theta_{i+1}\le\theta_i.}
\tag{R1}
\]

Assume instead that `theta_{i+1}>theta_i`. Then

\[
\alpha_i+\beta_{i+1}>\pi-\beta_i,
\]

so

\[
0\le\theta_i-\alpha_i
=\pi-\beta_i-\alpha_i
<\beta_{i+1}.
\tag{R2}
\]

Now `|x_i-p_{i+1}|=1`, `|p_{i+2}-p_{i+1}|=b_{i+1}`, and the angle between those two
vectors is `theta_i-alpha_i`. Hence

\[
|x_i-p_{i+2}|^2
=1+b_{i+1}^2
-2b_{i+1}\cos(\theta_i-\alpha_i).
\]

Because `theta_i-alpha_i<beta_{i+1}` and

\[
\cos\beta_{i+1}=b_{i+1}/2,
\]

we have

\[
\cos(\theta_i-\alpha_i)>b_{i+1}/2,
\]

and therefore

\[
|x_i-p_{i+2}|^2<1.
\]

This contradicts unit separation of the source points `x_i` and `p_{i+2}`. Thus (R1)
holds.

Consequently the smaller angular separation of the two apex rays is indeed

\[
\gamma_i
=\theta_i-\theta_{i+1}
=\pi-\alpha_i-\beta_i-\beta_{i+1}
\in[0,\pi).
\]

The distinct source points `x_i,x_{i+1}` both lie one unit from `p_{i+1}`, so unit
separation gives

\[
1\le|x_i-x_{i+1}|=2\sin(\gamma_i/2),
\]

hence `gamma_i>=pi/3`. Therefore the original theorem's inequality follows:

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_i
\le {2\pi\over3}-\beta_i-\beta_{i+1}
=f(e_i)+f(e_{i+1}).
}
\]

No additional hypothesis is introduced: `x_i` and `p_{i+2}` are already distinct
source points in the theorem setup, so their unit separation is part of the global
source assumptions.

**Audit correction.** Any audit of TT.1 must cite (R2) and the cross-distance
`|x_i-p_{i+2}|>=1`; “both rays lie in the common wedge, so their order is fixed” is
not a valid argument by itself.